{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.YoungDiagramCorner","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mathlib.YoungDiagramCorner","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Combinatorics.Young.YoungDiagram"],"declarations":4,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.card_eraseCorner","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.card_eraseCorner","kind":"theorem","line":60,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/YoungDiagramCorner.lean#L60-L67","doc":"The corner cell really is in `μ`, so removal drops the cell count by one. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.mem_eraseCorner","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.mem_eraseCorner","kind":"theorem","line":54,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/YoungDiagramCorner.lean#L54-L58","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.eraseCorner","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.eraseCorner","kind":"def","line":30,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/YoungDiagramCorner.lean#L30-L52","doc":"**Corner removal `μ ∖ c`.** Deleting a removable corner cell from `μ` yields a Young diagram:\n`cells.erase c` is still a lower set, because any `a ∈ μ` with `c ≤ a` and `a ≠ c` would have one of\n`c`'s (absent) neighbours `≤ a`, hence in `μ` by `up_left_mem` — contradicting that `c` is a corner. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.IsCornerCell","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.IsCornerCell","kind":"def","line":25,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/YoungDiagramCorner.lean#L25-L28","doc":"A **removable corner** of `μ`: a cell `c ∈ μ` whose right neighbour `(i, j+1)` and lower neighbour\n`(i+1, j)` are both outside `μ` — i.e. `c` is an outer corner whose deletion leaves a Young diagram. ","signatureChunk":0}]}